<section class="page-head">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Setup guide</p>
    <h1>Running a hackathon on Hackerly</h1>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:14px">
      Ten steps. Each one saves immediately, so you can stop and come back. You cannot publish
      until the pieces that participants and judges depend on are in place.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tight">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="ws">
      <div class="ws__main doc">
        <h2>1 · Name and describe it</h2>
        <p>
          The name, a one-line tagline and a description of at least 40 characters. The description
          is what a stranger reads before deciding to register, so write it for them, not for the
          people who already know you.
        </p>

        <h2>2 · Dates</h2>
        <p>
          Registration opening and closing, the build window, the submission deadline, the judging
          period and when results go out. Only the submission deadline is mandatory, because the
          server has to know when to refuse writes. Everything else you leave blank simply is not
          shown.
        </p>
        <p>
          All times are stored in UTC. The countdown on the public event page tells participants how
          long is left, and the server enforces the deadline itself — a stale browser tab or a
          crafted request gets the same answer.
        </p>

        <h2>3 · Prizes and eligibility</h2>
        <p>
          A prize pool with a currency, or a headline if you want to write “₹5,00,000 plus
          hardware”. Eligibility text appears on the event page, so use it for age or region limits
          if you have them.
        </p>

        <h2>4 · Teams</h2>
        <p>
          Minimum and maximum team size, and whether a solo entry counts. If you set a minimum above
          one, participants have to form a team before they can submit — the server enforces it
          rather than trusting the form.
        </p>

        <h2>5 · Tracks</h2>
        <p>
          Tracks are how you split a large field. Each has a name, a description, what a good entry
          looks like, and an optional prize. A track cannot be deleted once a project has used it,
          because the projects reference it.
        </p>

        <h2>6 · Submission fields</h2>
        <p>
          You decide what a submission contains: repository, demo, video, documentation,
          technologies, and any written questions you want answered. Mark the ones that are required
          and the server refuses an incomplete submission with a message naming what is missing.
        </p>

        <h2>7 · Judging</h2>
        <p>
          A rubric: named criteria, each with a weight and a maximum score. Weights must add up to
          100, or all be zero for equal weighting. Judges score against this rubric and nothing
          else — a request that names a criterion which is not in the rubric is rejected.
        </p>
        <p>
          You also choose the aggregation method: a plain mean of the reviews, or the documented
          normalization that removes panel harshness and scales inside each track. See
          <code>JUDGING.md</code>.
        </p>

        <h2>8 · Rules and schedule</h2>
        <p>
          Rules accept a small, safe subset of markdown: <code>- bullets</code>, numbered lists,
          <code>**bold**</code>, <code>`code`</code> and <code>&gt; quotes</code>. Everything else is
          escaped. The schedule is a list of milestones with a date and a note.
        </p>

        <h2>9 · Branding</h2>
        <p>
          A cover image URL, or a generated poster from six styles. One accent colour from a fixed
          palette, so an organizer cannot make their event unreadable. The Hackerly mark stays put.
        </p>

        <h2>10 · Review and publish</h2>
        <p>
          A preflight lists anything missing. Publishing makes the event public and opens discovery.
          After that you can go live, close submissions, judge, compute results and publish them.
        </p>

        <h2>Judging operations</h2>
        <p>
          Invite judges by email or paste a list. Each invitation is a single-use link tied to that
          address. Assign projects per judge, or let Hackerly balance the load across the panel.
          The judging page shows who is behind, which projects have no judge, and which judges have
          not accepted — all from the database, nothing hardcoded.
        </p>

        <h2>Results</h2>
        <p>
          Compute is separate from publish. Computing writes a private ranking; publishing releases
          it on the public event page along with whatever prizes you entered. You can withdraw a
          publication, and individual judges’ scores stay private to the panel either way.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="ws__side">
        <div class="card">
          <h2 class="card__title" style="margin-bottom:10px">Start now</h2>
          <a class="btn btn--accent btn--block" href="/host/events/new">Create a hackathon</a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--block" style="margin-top:8px" href="/host/events">Your events</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h2 class="card__title" style="margin-bottom:10px">Judging rules</h2>
          <p class="small muted">
            A judge cannot read another judge’s scores, and a participant cannot read any of them.
            The backend refuses both. Neither is enforced by hiding a button.
          </p>
          <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:10px">
            Exports respect the same rules: only organizers get CSV, and every export is written to
            the audit log.
          </p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
